+## ADR-010: LiteLLM Routing Deferred Until a Second Model Provider Route Exists (amends §131.11)
+
+* **Status:** `ACCEPTED`
+* **Date:** 2026-09-18
+* **Context:**
+  Spec §131.11 (MASTER_BUILD_SPEC.md:5886) states: "M1 decision: LiteLLM is the *routing implementation* behind the Model Gateway interface." Module 6 shipped a direct JARVIS-owned OpenAI-compatible adapter (`jarvis.providers.openai_compatible`) instead, disclosed only in its module docstring — a silent divergence flagged by the Freebuff module-6 red-team review (finding F7). ADR-001 is intact either way: the kernel imports no provider client; the adapter implements the `ProviderAdapter` Protocol.
+* **Decision:**
+  For M1, §131.11 is amended: the routing implementation behind the Model Gateway is the direct OpenAI-compatible adapter. LiteLLM is adopted at the moment a second provider route exists (second provider, multi-provider failover, or cross-provider budget routing) — whichever comes first — at which point it replaces the direct adapter behind the unchanged `ProviderAdapter` Protocol. Read §131.11 together with this ADR; the section's M3 revisit clause is unchanged.
+* **Consequences:**
+  - No kernel changes are required when LiteLLM lands (substitution seam holds by construction).
+  - The implementation no longer diverges from a decision "silently": the divergence is recorded, bounded, and has an explicit trigger.
+  - The provider-translation benefits §131.11 cites (key handling, retry) are deferred with it; the direct adapter must therefore keep transport failures mapped to `ProviderTransportError`.
